{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 2,
  "is_atomic": false,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of this analysis — are we maximizing the total value of exports (i.e., the value of goods Carelland sells to the world market)?",
    "Is the value measured at the world-market prices you listed (steel 500, engines 1500, electronics 300, plastic 1200 Klunz per unit)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for the objective (maximizing export value) and separately confirms the specific pricing basis for that value. These are distinct pieces of information that could be answered independently."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "How exactly is domestic GDP defined here — is it the total value of all four goods produced (steel, engines, electronics, plastic) valued at the world-market prices you listed, or is it the value of production minus the value of the imported goods used as inputs (i.e., net of the 250/300/50/300 Klunz imported-goods requirements per unit)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice between two mutually exclusive definitions of domestic GDP (gross vs. net of imports), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the four production input requirements you listed (e.g., engines require 0.8 steel, 0.15 electronic components, 0.11 plastic, 300 Klunz imported goods, 1 person-year labor) the complete set of inputs needed to produce one unit of each good, meaning the only constraints on production are the engine cap, the plastic cap, and the total labor cap — or are there additional constraints such as limited domestic availability of steel, electronic components, or plastic that also bound production?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking whether the listed inputs are the complete set of constraints or if there are additional binding constraints."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "How should these interdependencies be treated — are they simple linear coefficients where the total amount of each good consumed as input is just the sum over all production (so the model is a linear program), or do they represent a circular flow where the goods produced are themselves the inputs available for use (so production of one good must be net of what is consumed by the others)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice between two modeling approaches (linear coefficients vs. circular flow), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there any limit on how much imported goods Carelland can bring in (a budget or quota on total imports), or are imports unlimited and simply paid for at the listed prices?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the constraints on imported goods, presenting a binary choice between limited and unlimited imports."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
